    title: "How Are Dealership Detail Bays Designed for Water and Drainage?",
    description: "Learn dealership detail bay design: wash water supply, trench drains, oil-water separation, ventilation for chemical fumes, and defect-revealing lighting.",
    h1: "How Are Dealership Detail Bays Designed for Water and Drainage?",
    answer: "Dealership detail bays are designed as controlled wet environments where wash water, chemicals, and drainage are fully managed instead of running across the shop floor. A licensed engineer starts with the water system: tempered wash water supply with backflow prevention, hose bibbs or pressure-washer stations at each bay, and drainage graded so every drop flows to collection rather than to the parking lot or storm drain.\n\nDrainage is the heart of the design. Bay floors slope to trench drains or slot drains that capture wash water, which then flows through an oil-water separator before reaching the sanitary sewer — regulators do not allow petroleum-contaminated wash water into storm drains, and many jurisdictions require the separator by code. The engineer sizes the separator for the actual wash flow, details sand and sediment traps upstream, and provides cleanouts and access for the maintenance the separator will need.\n\nVentilation and finishes complete the bay. Detailing chemicals — dressings, solvents, odor bombs — release fumes that general shop ventilation should capture, and the bay's humidity load is higher than a dry service bay, so the engineer accounts for both. Floors get sealed, chemical-resistant coatings with integral coved bases, walls get washable finishes to splash height, and lighting is bright, high-CRI, and positioned so technicians can see swirls and defects in the paint they are correcting.",
    directAnswer: "Engineers design detail bays with tempered wash water, sloped floors to trench drains feeding an oil-water separator, chemical-resistant finishes, and ventilation and high-CRI lighting suited to wet detailing work.",
    topic: "Vehicle Dealerships",
    serviceHref: "/services/mep/",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Wash Water Supply and Backflow Protection", body: "Detail bays need reliable tempered water at every wash position. The engineer designs hot and cold supplies — often with a dedicated water heater for the detail operation — piped to hose stations or pressure-washer drops with quick connects, sized so simultaneous use does not collapse pressure. Water chemistry matters too: in hard-water regions, spot-free rinse systems or deionized final rinse prevent the mineral spotting that ruins a detail.\n\nBackflow prevention is a code requirement wherever hoses connect to the potable system, especially with chemical injectors in the mix. The design specifies the appropriate backflow assembly for the hazard level, locates it for testing access, and keeps chemical mixing stations separated from potable outlets so there is no cross-connection path." },
      { heading: "Trench Drains, Separators, and Floor Grading", body: "The floor is graded as a drainage system, not just a slab. Slopes direct wash water to trench drains running the length of each bay, and the trench drains feed a treatment train: sediment and sand traps first, then the oil-water separator, then the sanitary connection. The engineer sizes each element for peak wash flow and documents the maintenance access — separators that cannot be pumped out will fail.\n\nDischarge permitting shapes the design. Most jurisdictions prohibit wash water in storm drains outright and require separator-treated discharge to sanitary sewer under an industrial discharge permit, sometimes with sampling ports. The engineer confirms the local requirements early, because a detail bay designed without the permit path becomes a very expensive retrofit." },
      { heading: "Detail Bay Design Checklist", body: "A complete detail bay package manages water from supply to sewer:\n\n• Tempered wash water with backflow prevention at every hose station\n• Floor grading to trench drains sized for peak simultaneous wash flow\n• Sediment traps and oil-water separator ahead of the sanitary connection\n• Discharge permit path confirmed with the local authority\n• Chemical-resistant sealed floors with coved bases and washable wall finishes\n• Ventilation for chemical fumes and elevated humidity\n• High-CRI lighting positioned for paint defect visibility" },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: "Why can't detail bay wash water go to the storm drain?", answer: "Wash water carries petroleum residues, detergents, heavy metals from brake dust, and detailing chemicals — all pollutants that storm drains carry untreated to waterways. Environmental regulations prohibit the discharge, and violations bring fines. The engineered solution routes wash water through trench drains and an oil-water separator to the sanitary sewer, where it receives treatment. The design also includes the local discharge permit, which many jurisdictions require before the bay opens." },
      { question: "How is an oil-water separator sized for a detail bay?", answer: "By the peak wash water flow it must treat — the number of bays washing simultaneously, hose flow rates, and any pressure-washer demand — plus the expected oil loading. Gravity separators need enough residence time for oil droplets to float and separate, so undersizing means oil passes straight through. The engineer sizes from the plumbing fixture count and flow rates, adds sediment pretreatment, and specifies a unit with accessible chambers for the regular pump-outs it will need." },
      { question: "What flooring works in a dealership detail bay?", answer: "Sealed, chemical-resistant flooring — typically an epoxy or polyaspartic system rated for petroleum and detergent exposure — with integral coved bases that carry the waterproofing up the wall. The surface needs enough texture for wet slip resistance without being so rough it traps grime. Control joints are sealed, and the whole assembly is graded to drains. Bare concrete absorbs oils and chemicals, stains permanently, and eventually breaks down under constant wet chemical exposure." },
      { question: "Do detail bays need special lighting?", answer: "Yes — defect visibility is the point of detailing. The design uses bright, high-CRI lighting positioned at angles that reveal swirls, holograms, and paint defects rather than washing them out with flat overhead light. Many shops add color-tunable or inspection lighting at the final quality station so the finish reads correctly before delivery. Standard shop high-bays alone leave technicians chasing defects they cannot see." },
    ],
    extraLinks: [
      { label: "How Are Car Wash Facilities Designed and Engineered?", href: "/answers/car-wash-design/" },
      { label: "How Are Trench Drains Designed for Commercial Sites?", href: "/answers/trench-drain-design/" },
      { label: "How Are Grease Interceptors Designed for Commercial Kitchens?", href: "/answers/grease-interceptor-design/" },
      { label: "Get an engineering estimate", href: "/estimate" },
    ],
